{"test_cases_lookup_map": {"{\"actual_output\": \"The Reserve Bank\\u2019s **Pre\\u2011payment Charges on Loans Directions,\\u202f2025** (effective for all floating\\u2011rate loans and advances sanctioned or renewed on\\u202for after\\u202f1\\u202fJanuary\\u202f2026) prohibit lenders from levying any pre\\u2011payment penalty in two situations:\\n\\n1. **Loans to individuals for non\\u2011business purposes** \\u2013 no pre\\u2011payment charge may be imposed, irrespective of the lender type.  \\n2. **Business\\u2011purpose loans to individuals and micro\\u2011small enterprises (MSEs)** \\u2013 pre\\u2011payment charges are barred **provided the loan amount does not exceed the sector\\u2011specific ceiling (e.g., up to\\u202f\\u20b950\\u202flakh for Small Finance Banks, Regional Rural Banks, Rural Co\\u2011operative Banks, NBFC\\u2011Middle\\u2011Layer entities and Tier\\u20113 Urban Co\\u2011operative Banks).**\\n\\nThe measure is intended to make credit cheaper and more transparent for households and MSEs, eliminate restrictive clauses that generate borrower grievances, and promote uniform, affordable financing across the banking system.\", \"context\": null, \"expected_output\": \"no pre-payment charges on loans granted to individuals for non-business purposes and no pre-payment charges on loans granted by specified categories of REs for business purposes to individuals and MSEs subject to the threshold limit\", \"hyperparameters\": null, \"input\": \"What is the domestic policy saying about prepayment of loans?\", \"retrieval_context\": [\"## 3.10 Pre-payment Charges on Loans\\n\\nIII.27 To ensure affordable and transparent financing for micro and small enterprises (MSEs) and prevent divergent practices and restrictive clauses by REs that cause customer grievances, the Reserve Bank issued the Prepayment Charges on Loans Directions, 2025 on July 2, 2025, to be applicable in case of loans and advances sanctioned or renewed on or after January 1, 2026. Under these Directions, the REs shall, inter alia, adhere to the following Directions for floating rate loans and advances: (i) no pre-payment charges on loans granted to individuals for non-business purposes; and (ii)\\n\\nno pre-payment charges on loans granted by specified categories of REs for business purposes to individuals and MSEs subject to the threshold limit (e.g., loans up to \\u20b950 lakh for small finance banks (SFBs), RRBs, rural co-operative banks (RCBs), NBFCs-Middle Layer and Tier 3 urban co-operative banks (UCBs)).\\n\\n## 3.11 Investment in Alternative Investment Fund\", \"## Lending to Related Parties\\n\\nI.11 The Reserve Bank issued a principlebased related-party lending draft framework on October 3, 2025, to rationalise the existing provisions. The major changes proposed in the draft framework include introduction of scalebased materiality thresholds beyond which lending to related parties of an RE shall need approval of the Board or its Committee and suitable supervisory reporting and disclosure requirements on transaction with related parties.\\n\\n## Review of Investment Fluctuation Reserve\\n\\nI.12 Banks are required to maintain an investment fluctuation reserve (IFR) to provide them an additional buffer against depreciation in\\n\\n6 Transactors mean obligors in relation to facilities such as credit cards and charge cards where the balance has been repaid in full at each scheduled repayment date for the previous 12 months.\\n\\n\\n\\n<!-- PDF BATCH 26-30 -->\", \"<!-- PDF BATCH 56-60 -->\\n\\nretention by the originating lender from 20 per cent to 10 per cent; (iii) permitting default loss guarantee cover up to 5 per cent of outstanding loans; and (iv) mandating transfer of loan exposures within 15 days to avoid regulatory arbitrage. The Directions also strengthen borrower safeguards and market discipline. Collectively, these measures aim to encourage growth of the co-lending market while improving underwriting standards, ensuring transparency, and preventing misuse of co-lending structures.\\n\\n## 3.13 Non-Fund Based Credit Facilities\", \"III.15 To establish a harmonised framework for financing of projects in infrastructure and noninfrastructure (including commercial real estate and commercial real estate-residential housing) sectors by REs, the Reserve Bank issued the Project Finance Directions on June 19, 2025, which have subsequently been subsumed under the consolidated Directions issued on November 28, 2025. The Directions adopt a principle-based approach to resolution of stress with stronger risk safeguards, while balancing flexibility for lenders. Key measures include rationalised timelines for extension of the date of commencement of commercial operations - up to three years for infrastructure projects and two years for non-infrastructure projects - along with calibrated standard asset provisioning starting with 1 per cent for under-construction projects (1.25 per cent for commercial real estate), which increases with each quarter of deferment. Overall, the framework seeks to support timely and disciplined\", \"III.26 To further the objective of reducing the stock of unclaimed deposits and fresh accretion to the Depositor Education and Awareness (DEA) Fund, the Reserve Bank, on September 30, 2025, launched the Scheme for Facilitating Accelerated Payout - Inoperative Accounts and Unclaimed Deposits, for a period of one year from October 1, 2025 to September 30, 2026. During this period, banks will be eligible for differential pay-out based on period of inoperativeness of account and amount of unclaimed deposits, for the inoperative accounts reactivated and unclaimed deposits paid to rightful claimants.\\n\\n## 3.10 Pre-payment Charges on Loans\"]}": {"cached_metrics_data": [{"metric_data": {"name": "Faithfulness", "threshold": 0.7, "success": false, "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "Groq Judge", "evaluationCost": 0.0}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.7, "evaluation_model": "Groq Judge", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": true}}, {"metric_data": {"name": "Answer Relevancy", "threshold": 0.7, "success": false, "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "Groq Judge", "evaluationCost": 0.0}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.7, "evaluation_model": "Groq Judge", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": true}}, {"metric_data": {"name": "Contextual Recall", "threshold": 0.7, "success": false, "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "Groq Judge", "evaluationCost": 0.0}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.7, "evaluation_model": "Groq Judge", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": true}}, {"metric_data": {"name": "Contextual Precision", "threshold": 0.7, "success": false, "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "Groq Judge", "evaluationCost": 0.0}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.7, "evaluation_model": "Groq Judge", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": true}}], "cached_classifications": []}, "{\"actual_output\": \"Based on the table that lists **gross\\u2011NPA balances for Public\\u2011Sector Banks (PSBs)**, the only quantitative change shown for the fiscal year\\u202f2024\\u201125 is the **addition of \\u20b9\\u202f82,762\\u202fcrore** to the opening balance of \\u20b9\\u202f339,541\\u202fcrore.  \\n\\nThe table does **not provide the closing\\u2011balance figure for 2024\\u201125**, nor does it give a separate line for \\u201creductions\\u201d (recoveries, write\\u2011offs, up\\u2011gradations, etc.). Consequently, the **net reduction in gross NPAs** for PSBs during 2024\\u201125 cannot be calculated from the data supplied. The information available allows us to state the opening balance and the amount added, but the total reduced value remains indeterminate without the closing\\u2011balance or explicit reduction figure.\", \"context\": null, \"expected_output\": \"138653 crore.\", \"hyperparameters\": null, \"input\": \"What is the total reduced value of gross NPAs for PSBs during 2024-25? \", \"retrieval_context\": [\"- Notes: 1. Data for SCBs exclude RRBs and PBs.\\n\\n2. Closing balance for 2023-24 is not equal to opening balance for 2024-25 due to merger of one SFB into another effective April 1, 2024. The balance NPA pertaining to the merged SFB- amounting to \\u20b9199.54 crores - has been incorporated under GNPA additions during the year 2024-25.\\n3. Net NPAs=Gross NPA - deductions; net advances=gross advances - deductions. Deductions include: i) provisions held in the case of NPA accounts as per asset classification; ii) DICGC / ECGC claims received and held pending adjustment; iii) part payment received and kept in suspense account or any other similar account; iv) balance in sundry account (interest capitalisation - restructured accounts), in respect of NPA accounts; and v) floating provisions.\\n4. Constituent items may not add up to the total due to rounding off.\\n\\nSources: Annual accounts of banks and off-site returns (global operations), RBI.\", \"Note: Data for SCBs exclude RRBs and PBs.\\n\\nSource: Off-site returns, RBI.\\n\\nat end-March 2025 from 2.7 per cent at endMarch 2024. During 2024-25, around 42.8 per cent of the reduction in GNPAs was attributable to recoveries and upgradations. The net NPA (NNPA) ratio also declined to 0.5 per cent at endMarch 2025, partly reflecting higher provisioning (Table IV.9). As per supervisory data, at endSeptember 2025, the GNPA and NNPA ratios of SCBs stood at 2.1 per cent and 0.5 per cent, respectively.\\n\\nIV.30 The slippage ratio of SCBs, which measures new accretions to NPAs as a share of standard advances at the beginning of the year, declined for the fifth consecutive year to 1.4 per cent at end-March 2025. Slippage ratio of both PSBs and PVBs declined, although it remained higher for PVBs (Charts IV.16a and b). For SCBs,\\n\\n## Table IV.8: Net Stable Funding Ratio\\n\\n(At end-March 2025)\", \"P: Provisional.\\n\\nNotes: 1. Figures in parentheses are the proportion to total income/ expenditure (in per cent).\\n\\n2. Components may not add up to the total due to rounding off. Source: NABARD.\\n\\n<!-- image -->\\n\\n## Asset Quality\\n\\nV.39. The asset quality of StCBs improved for the fourth consecutive year, with the GNPA ratio declining to 4.8 per cent at end-March 2025 from 6.7 per cent at end-March 2021. The share of doubtful assets in total NPAs reduced further to 50.5 per cent from 56.7 per cent a year ago (Table V.16). Reflecting the moderation in GNPA and the provision coverage ratio during the year, net NPA ratio remained stable at 2.0 per cent. Except the central region, the GNPA ratio declined across all other regions (Appendix Table V.3).\\n\\n## Capital Adequacy\", \"| 1                                         | PSBs - 2   | PVBs - 3   |   FBs - 4 |   (Amount in \\u20b9 crore) - SFBs - 5 | (Amount in \\u20b9 crore) - SCBs - 6   |\\n|-------------------------------------------|------------|------------|-----------|----------------------------------|----------------------------------|\\n| Gross NPAs                                |            |            |           |                                  |                                  |\\n| Closing Balance for 2023-24               | 3,39,541   | 1,29,164   |     6,523 |                            5,590 | 4,80,818                         |\\n| Opening Balance for 2024-25               | 3,39,541   | 1,29,164   |     6,523 |                            5,391 | 4,80,619                         |\\n| Addition during the year 2024-25          | 82,762     | 1,21,735   |     7,256 |                           14,607 | 2,26,359                         |\", \"| Closing Balance for 2024-25               | 2,83,650   | 1,32,674   |     5,321 |                            9,989 | 4,31,634                         |\\n| Gross NPAs as per cent of Gross Advances* |            |            |           |                                  |                                  |\\n| 2023-24                                   | 3.5        | 1.9        |       1.2 |                              2.4 | 2.7                              |\\n| 2024-25                                   | 2.6        | 1.8        |       0.9 |                              3.6 | 2.2                              |\\n| Net NPAs                                  |            |            |           |                                  |                                  |\\n| 2023-24                                   | 72,544     | 31,594     |       812 |                            1,796 | 1,06,745                         |\"]}": {"cached_metrics_data": [{"metric_data": {"name": "Contextual Recall", "threshold": 0.7, "success": false, "score": 0.0, "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "Groq Judge", "evaluationCost": 0.0}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.7, "evaluation_model": "Groq Judge", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": true}}, {"metric_data": {"name": "Contextual Precision", "threshold": 0.7, "success": false, "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "Groq Judge", "evaluationCost": 0.0}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.7, "evaluation_model": "Groq Judge", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": true}}], "cached_classifications": []}, "{\"actual_output\": \"The Reserve Bank\\u2019s **Pre\\u2011payment Charges on Loans Directions, 2025** (issued\\u202f2\\u202fJuly\\u202f2025, effective for loans sanctioned or renewed on\\u202for after\\u202f1\\u202fJanuary\\u202f2026) prohibit lenders from levying pre\\u2011payment penalties in the following cases:\\n\\n1. **Floating\\u2011rate loans to individuals for non\\u2011business purposes** \\u2013 no pre\\u2011payment charge may be imposed.  \\n2. **Floating\\u2011rate business loans to individuals and micro\\u2011small enterprises (MSEs)** \\u2013 pre\\u2011payment charges are barred when the loan is extended by the specified categories of regulated entities (small\\u2011finance banks, regional rural banks, rural co\\u2011operative banks, middle\\u2011layer NBFCs and Tier\\u20113 urban co\\u2011operative banks) **and the amount does not exceed the prescribed ceiling (e.g., up to\\u202f\\u20b950\\u202flakh).**\\n\\nThe policy aims to make credit cheaper and more transparent for consumers and MSEs, eliminate divergent or restrictive pre\\u2011payment clauses, and reduce customer grievances. All other lenders and loan types not covered by the above exemptions remain subject to any pre\\u2011payment terms they may set, subject to general RBI prudential guidelines.\", \"context\": null, \"expected_output\": \"no pre-payment charges on loans granted to individuals for non-business purposes and no pre-payment charges on loans granted by specified categories of REs for business purposes to individuals and MSEs subject to the threshold limit\", \"hyperparameters\": null, \"input\": \"What is the domestic policy saying about prepayment of loans?\", \"retrieval_context\": [\"## 3.10 Pre-payment Charges on Loans\\n\\nIII.27 To ensure affordable and transparent financing for micro and small enterprises (MSEs) and prevent divergent practices and restrictive clauses by REs that cause customer grievances, the Reserve Bank issued the Prepayment Charges on Loans Directions, 2025 on July 2, 2025, to be applicable in case of loans and advances sanctioned or renewed on or after January 1, 2026. Under these Directions, the REs shall, inter alia, adhere to the following Directions for floating rate loans and advances: (i) no pre-payment charges on loans granted to individuals for non-business purposes; and (ii)\\n\\nno pre-payment charges on loans granted by specified categories of REs for business purposes to individuals and MSEs subject to the threshold limit (e.g., loans up to \\u20b950 lakh for small finance banks (SFBs), RRBs, rural co-operative banks (RCBs), NBFCs-Middle Layer and Tier 3 urban co-operative banks (UCBs)).\\n\\n## 3.11 Investment in Alternative Investment Fund\", \"## Lending to Related Parties\\n\\nI.11 The Reserve Bank issued a principlebased related-party lending draft framework on October 3, 2025, to rationalise the existing provisions. The major changes proposed in the draft framework include introduction of scalebased materiality thresholds beyond which lending to related parties of an RE shall need approval of the Board or its Committee and suitable supervisory reporting and disclosure requirements on transaction with related parties.\\n\\n## Review of Investment Fluctuation Reserve\\n\\nI.12 Banks are required to maintain an investment fluctuation reserve (IFR) to provide them an additional buffer against depreciation in\\n\\n6 Transactors mean obligors in relation to facilities such as credit cards and charge cards where the balance has been repaid in full at each scheduled repayment date for the previous 12 months.\\n\\n\\n\\n<!-- PDF BATCH 26-30 -->\", \"<!-- PDF BATCH 56-60 -->\\n\\nretention by the originating lender from 20 per cent to 10 per cent; (iii) permitting default loss guarantee cover up to 5 per cent of outstanding loans; and (iv) mandating transfer of loan exposures within 15 days to avoid regulatory arbitrage. The Directions also strengthen borrower safeguards and market discipline. Collectively, these measures aim to encourage growth of the co-lending market while improving underwriting standards, ensuring transparency, and preventing misuse of co-lending structures.\\n\\n## 3.13 Non-Fund Based Credit Facilities\", \"III.15 To establish a harmonised framework for financing of projects in infrastructure and noninfrastructure (including commercial real estate and commercial real estate-residential housing) sectors by REs, the Reserve Bank issued the Project Finance Directions on June 19, 2025, which have subsequently been subsumed under the consolidated Directions issued on November 28, 2025. The Directions adopt a principle-based approach to resolution of stress with stronger risk safeguards, while balancing flexibility for lenders. Key measures include rationalised timelines for extension of the date of commencement of commercial operations - up to three years for infrastructure projects and two years for non-infrastructure projects - along with calibrated standard asset provisioning starting with 1 per cent for under-construction projects (1.25 per cent for commercial real estate), which increases with each quarter of deferment. Overall, the framework seeks to support timely and disciplined\", \"III.26 To further the objective of reducing the stock of unclaimed deposits and fresh accretion to the Depositor Education and Awareness (DEA) Fund, the Reserve Bank, on September 30, 2025, launched the Scheme for Facilitating Accelerated Payout - Inoperative Accounts and Unclaimed Deposits, for a period of one year from October 1, 2025 to September 30, 2026. During this period, banks will be eligible for differential pay-out based on period of inoperativeness of account and amount of unclaimed deposits, for the inoperative accounts reactivated and unclaimed deposits paid to rightful claimants.\\n\\n## 3.10 Pre-payment Charges on Loans\"]}": {"cached_metrics_data": [{"metric_data": {"name": "Contextual Recall", "threshold": 0.7, "success": true, "score": 1.0, "reason": "The score is 1.00 because the expected output is fully supported by node 2 in the retrieval context, which contains the exact phrase about no pre\u2011payment charges for the specified loan categories.", "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "Groq Judge", "evaluationCost": 0.0, "verboseLogs": "Verdicts:\n[\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": \"2nd node contains the phrase: ...no pre-payment charges on loans granted to individuals for non-business purposes; and (ii) no pre-payment charges on loans granted by specified categories of REs for business purposes to individuals and MSEs subject to the threshold limit...\",\n        \"expected_output\": \"no pre-payment charges on loans granted to individuals for non-business purposes and no pre-payment charges on loans granted by specified categories of REs for business purposes to individuals and MSEs subject to the threshold limit\"\n    }\n]"}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.7, "evaluation_model": "Groq Judge", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": true}}, {"metric_data": {"name": "Contextual Precision", "threshold": 0.7, "success": false, "score": 0.5, "reason": "The score is 0.50 because the only relevant node (node 2) is ranked second, while nodes 1, 3, 4, and 5 are irrelevant and appear above it. For example, node 1 states \"The document discusses retention rates, default loss guarantees, and loan exposure transfers, but contains no mention of pre\u2011payment charges on loans.\" and node 3 says \"The document focuses on related\u2011party lending frameworks and investment fluctuation reserves, with no reference to pre\u2011payment charges.\" These irrelevant nodes are placed higher than the relevant node that contains the policy text \"no pre\u2011payment charges on loans granted to individuals for non\u2011business purposes; and no pre\u2011payment charges on loans granted by specified categories of REs for business purposes to individuals and MSEs subject to the threshold limit (e.g., loans up to \u20b950 lakh for small finance banks (SFBs), RRBs, rural co\u2011operative banks (RCBs), NBFCs\u2011Middle Layer and Tier 3 urban co\u2011operative banks (UCBs)).\" Thus, only half of the relevant nodes are ranked above irrelevant ones, yielding a precision of 0.50.", "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "Groq Judge", "evaluationCost": 0.0, "verboseLogs": "Verdicts:\n[\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"no\",\n        \"reason\": \"The document discusses retention rates, default loss guarantees, and loan exposure transfers, but contains no mention of pre\\u2011payment charges on loans. For example, it states \\\"retention by the originating lender from 20 per cent to 10 per cent; (iii) permitting default loss guarantee cover up to 5 per cent of outstanding loans; and (iv) mandating transfer of loan exposures within 15 days\\u2026\\\".\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"yes\",\n        \"reason\": \"This document explicitly contains the policy text that matches the expected output: \\\"no pre\\u2011payment charges on loans granted to individuals for non\\u2011business purposes; and no pre\\u2011payment charges on loans granted by specified categories of REs for business purposes to individuals and MSEs subject to the threshold limit (e.g., loans up to \\u20b950 lakh for small finance banks (SFBs), RRBs, rural co\\u2011operative banks (RCBs), NBFCs\\u2011Middle Layer and Tier 3 urban co\\u2011operative banks (UCBs)).\\\"\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"no\",\n        \"reason\": \"The document focuses on related\\u2011party lending frameworks and investment fluctuation reserves, with no reference to pre\\u2011payment charges. It states \\\"The Reserve Bank issued a principlebased related\\u2011party lending draft framework\\u2026\\\" and discusses IFR requirements, but nothing about pre\\u2011payment.\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"no\",\n        \"reason\": \"Although the heading \\\"## 3.10 Pre\\u2011payment Charges on Loans\\\" appears, the document provides no substantive details about the policy. It only mentions the heading and then shifts to a scheme for unclaimed deposits.\"\n    },\n    {\n        \"verdict\": \"no\",\n        \"reason\": \"This document discusses project finance directions and infrastructure financing, with no mention of pre\\u2011payment charges on loans. It states \\\"To establish a harmonised framework for financing of projects in infrastructure and noninfrastructure\\u2026\\\" but does not address pre\\u2011payment.\"\n    }\n]"}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.7, "evaluation_model": "Groq Judge", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": true}}], "cached_classifications": []}, "{\"actual_output\": \"**Singapore \\u2013 MAS Stablecoin Regulatory Framework (2023)**  \\n\\nThe Monetary Authority of Singapore (MAS) introduced a dedicated framework for stablecoins in 2023. It applies **only to single\\u2011currency stablecoins** that are **pegged to the Singapore dollar or any G10 fiat currency** and that are **issued within Singapore**. Under the framework, issuers must be **licensed or regulated entities** (e.g., banks, capital\\u2011market firms, or other MAS\\u2011authorized participants) and are required to **maintain robust governance, risk\\u2011management and AML/CFT controls**. The framework mandates **full backing of the stablecoin with highly liquid assets** to ensure redemption at par, and it imposes **transparent disclosure obligations** regarding the asset\\u2011backing, redemption mechanisms and any fees. Ongoing supervisory oversight by MAS includes periodic reporting, stress\\u2011testing of liquidity buffers, and the ability to intervene if the stablecoin poses systemic or consumer\\u2011protection risks. This regime aims to safeguard investors while fostering responsible innovation in Singapore\\u2019s digital\\u2011finance ecosystem.\", \"context\": null, \"expected_output\": \"MAS 2023 is the regulation and is applicable to single currency stablecoins pegged to singapore dollar or any G10 currency\", \"hyperparameters\": null, \"input\": \"What are the Regulations for Stablecoins in Singapore and their key features?\", \"retrieval_context\": [\"## 3.8 Crypto-assets\\n\\nII.22 Stablecoins - crypto-assets designed to maintain a stable value by referencing one or more fiat currencies - have become a key component of the digital asset ecosystem. The market capitalisation of stablecoins reached approximately US$ 300 billion at end-September 2025. 15 The increasing adoption of stablecoins raises concerns for both monetary policy and financial stability, particularly in EMDEs as their widespread use can weaken central banks' control over money supply and interest rates. Proliferation of stablecoins also creates risk of currency substitution, weakening the effect of monetary policy transmission and banking disintermediation. 16\", \"II.24 Stablecoins are evolving under formal regulatory oversight, and many countries have put in place stablecoin regulations to safeguard investors (Table II.1). The FSB released a followup report of its global regulatory framework governing crypto-asset activities in November 2025. The report revealed that while progress has been made in regulating crypto-asset activities and global stablecoins, significant gaps remain. Global stablecoin regulation is fragmented, and cross-border coordination is inadequate, hindering effective oversight and timely responses to systemic risks. 22\\n\\n## 3.9 Artificial Intelligence and Tokenisation\", \"II.26 At end-2024, the Irving Fisher Committee on Central Bank Statistics conducted a survey covering six dimensions to assess the current state of AI adoption: (i) scope and interest; (ii) expectations; (iii) applications; (iv) organisational policies, governance and risks; (v) IT stack; and (vi) collaborative strategies. 25 The survey highlighted two key findings: first,\\n\\n17 The Guiding and Establishing National Innovation for US Stablecoins (GENIUS) Act. Stablecoin Legislation: An Overview of the GENIUS Act of 2025, Library of Congress, USA.\\n\\n18 http://esma.europa.eu/esmas-activities/digital-finance-and-innovation/markets-crypto-assets-regulation-mica.\\n\\n19 https://www.sgpc.gov.sg/api/file/getfile/Media%20Release\\\\_MAS%20Finalises%20Stablecoin%20Regulatory%20Framework.pdf?path=/sgpcmedia/ media\\\\_releases/mas/press\\\\_release/P-20230815-2/attachment/Media%20Release\\\\_MAS%20Finalises%20Stablecoin%20Regulatory%20Framework.pdf\\n\\n20 https://www.fsa.go.jp/en/news/2022/20220914-2/02.pdf.\", \"| Japan         | Regulatory Framework for Crypto-assets and Stablecoins 20                         | Only banks, fund transfer service providers, and trust companies are entitled to issue digital-money type stablecoins.                                    |\", \"| United States | Guiding and Establishing National Innovation for U.S. Stablecoins (GENIUS) Act 17 | Dual federal-state regulation with 100 per cent backup in terms of issuers' holding of highly liquid assets.                                              |\\n| EU            | Markets in Crypto-Assets (MiCA) Regulation, 2024 18                               | Classifies stablecoins into two categories - asset-referenced tokens and e-money tokens. Issuers must obtain licenses to operate within EU jurisdictions. |\\n| Singapore     | Monetary Authority of Singapore (MAS) Stablecoin Regulatory Framework, 2023 19    | Applicable to single-currency stablecoins, pegged to the Singapore dollar or any G10 currency, that is issued in Singapore.                               |\"]}": {"cached_metrics_data": [{"metric_data": {"name": "Contextual Recall", "threshold": 0.7, "success": false, "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "Groq Judge", "evaluationCost": 0.0}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.7, "evaluation_model": "Groq Judge", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": true}}, {"metric_data": {"name": "Contextual Precision", "threshold": 0.7, "success": false, "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "Groq Judge", "evaluationCost": 0.0}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.7, "evaluation_model": "Groq Judge", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": true}}], "cached_classifications": []}, "{\"actual_output\": \"**Regulation:** Monetary Authority of Singapore (MAS) Stablecoin Regulatory Framework, 2023.  \\n\\n**Key features:**  \\n- **Scope:** Applies only to single\\u2011currency stablecoins that are pegged to the Singapore dollar (SGD) or any G10 fiat currency and are issued within Singapore.  \\n- **Issuer eligibility:** The framework i